  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">NIHR DA 1: A community based intervention against harmful use of alcohol in rural based settings</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">A low- and middle-income country (LMIC) award led by Makerere University, Uganda in partnership with Ugandan institutions: Mbarara University and Kabale University as well as Southampton University in the UK. Research activity is focused on conducting a pilot study which aims to provide an evidence base for a larger research proposal. 

The Ugandan sub-region and location of the research is plagued with high levels of alcohol abuse that has affected health and productivity, and is associated notably with increasing poverty and domestic violence. Commencing in January 2025, the focus of the 12-month award is on the development, piloting and evaluation of a community-based peer support intervention to reduce the problem of alcohol use in Uganda. The study involves a systematic review of literature on intervention studies against alcohol abuse in rural settings, a survey on alcohol consumption patterns and a qualitative study of perceptions towards alcohol consumption.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The award has three objectives:

1. To review existing evidence on community-based interventions for reducing harmful alcohol use in rural areas of low- and middle-income countries (LMICs), their effectiveness in different contexts, and potential mechanisms.
2. To conduct a survey to establish the level of harmful use of alcohol and factors associated with the behaviour.
3. To explore reasons for harmful use of alcohol, and perceptions on effective and acceptable ways of reducing the alcohol abuse.</narrative>
  </description>
